25, -0.00391387939453125, -0.04254150390625, 0.016571044921875, -0.0209808349609375, -0.007137298583984375, -0.042144775390625, -0.0283203125, -0.024261474609375, -0.04107666015625, 0.02679443359375, 0.0273590087890625, 0.0157012939453125, 0.0177459716796875, 0.00701904296875, -0.00412750244140625, 0.00970458984375, -0.0599365234375, 0.01885986328125, 0.003353118896484375, 0.0197906494140625, 0.034759521484375, -0.03662109375, -0.0135345458984375, -0.0208740234375, -0.0168304443359375, 0.0002853870391845703, 0.01134490966796875, 0.002899169921875, 0.0200042724609375, 0.01158905029296875, 0.00540924072265625, 1.1920928955078125e-06, 0.015838623046875, -0.003742218017578125, -0.00632476806640625, 0.00409698486328125, 0.01355743408203125, 0.012664794921875, -0.03369140625, 0.0022640228271484375, -0.005100250244140625, -0.0335693359375, -0.01617431640625, -0.001255035400390625, 0.0122222900390625, -0.0281524658203125, 0.0115509033203125, -0.01343536376953125, 0.0122222900390625, -0.0020904541015625, 0.001682281494140625, -0.0005297660827636719, 0.0053558349609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. To investigate these issues, we use the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) to identify level-0 actions and beliefs in both the canonical beauty contest game and a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), where observed behavior appears inconsistent with the standard level-k model. In the beauty contest game, we replicate Burchardi and Penczynski\u2019s (2014) finding that elicited level-0 beliefs closely align with observed level-0 actions. However, in the variant of the 11-20 game, elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions diverge from each other and from the standard level-0 assumption. This divergence offers an alternative explanation for the behavioral patterns documented by Goeree et al. (2018)."}}